75: \begin{document}
76: 
77: \maketitle
78: 
79: \begin{abstract}
80: Sequential confidence in a contextual bandit can use features frozen when
81: rewards are collected, while retraining changes the features used by current
82: curvature. We study the guarantees and limitations of transporting confidence
83: to this relinearized curvature. A conditional UCB theorem separates metric
84: transport, approximate operators, and solver widths, and bounds regret through
85: the frozen sequential information gain. For squared loss, a corrected
86: prediction center supplies reference confidence along predictable parameter
87: paths in a certified region, with explicit Taylor and misspecification error
88: envelopes and no nonlinear optimizer-convergence requirement. The certified
89: width cannot be smaller than the direct reference width on the same history;
90: exact actionwise transport reproduces that width. This comparison does not order the realized regret of policies
91: with different histories. We examine these distinctions in a 29-parameter
92: scaled-tanh model, for which a relative-weight comparison also supplies a
93: horizon-independent endpoint certificate. A prespecified follow-up evaluates
94: four policies across three conditions with 50 paired seeds per condition. The
95: Hessian-transport policy has higher mean pseudo-regret than every comparator
96: in every condition; all nine pointwise paired confidence intervals exclude
97: zero. Its regret bounds are vacuous and dominated by path inflation, while the
98: reference policy's own bounds are also vacuous. Floating-point confidence and
99: optimism diagnostics pass for all evaluated policies, including an uncertified
100: control. Exploratory log analyses distinguish conditional validity,
101: certificate usefulness, and observed policy behavior in this implementation.
102: \end{abstract}
